{
 "id": "8.6",
 "chapter": "8",
 "title": "Sub-Doppler cooling",
 "short": "Sub-Doppler cooling",
 "lead": "Why atoms cool below the <span data-concept=\"doppler-limit\">Doppler limit</span> in two beams of crossed linear polarization: on a J = 1/2 → 3/2 transition the <span data-concept=\"polarization-gradient\">polarization gradient</span> gives the two ground sublevels <span data-concept=\"ground-state-light-shift-potential\">light-shift hills</span> a quarter wavelength apart, and <span data-concept=\"optical-pumping\">optical pumping</span> that lags the moving atom makes it climb more than it descends; the friction this gives, its <span data-concept=\"capture-range\">capture velocity</span>, and the temperature where it stops.",
